<main class="full-view help-view">
  <div class="eyebrow">Storyweaver {version}</div><h1>A novel you can guide</h1>
  <p>Begin with an idea, shape an ensemble and a world, then guide an ongoing illustrated story. You can follow any cast member or let the narrator choose.</p>
  <h2>Start your first story</h2>
  <ol><li>In Settings → Text LLM, connect your OpenAI-compatible server, choose a model, save, and run a test prompt.</li><li>In Settings → ComfyUI, test your server, choose or import an API workflow, check its bindings, and generate a test picture. Pictures are optional while reading.</li><li>Choose New story, describe your idea, generate the cast/world and review the setup. Begin chapter one when it looks right.</li><li>Continue adds a passage to the chapter. Give a direction or leave it open. Passage and chapter length targets are editable in Story settings.</li></ol>
  <div class="row"><button class="primary" onclick={onNew}>Begin a new story</button><button onclick={onSettings}>Connection settings</button></div>
  <p>Every setup section has AI options. Revise or fully redesign individual records, reroll names/titles, or generate new places, possessions, relationships and story threads. World foundations and the opening plan can be revised separately. Give a direction to keep specific details. Current edits save first; results stay editable. The opening plan is a brief—Begin chapter one writes the prose.</p>
  <h2>Read, direct and edit</h2>
  <p>Workflows shows each passage’s recorded stages, model requests, selected records and check results. Use the stage list or read-only diagram to inspect a failure. Correct saved records in the existing editors, then retry world updates from retained prose and review before saving. Unsubmitted guidance and later saved changes prevent an older proposal from being accepted. Stored details stay on this computer; remove them from workflow history when no draft or dependent run needs them.</p>
  <p>Use Background in a written story to edit setting details, optional routines, keywords, linked records and visibility. Hidden entries are visible to you as the author and stay out of narration. Save background edits explicitly. Dev → Lore adds diagnostics and measured context-selection reasons.</p>
  <p>Library, Contents, the notebook, setup sections and Records have search controls for larger stories. Searching keeps your current record draft. In the world notebook, the first twelve possessions are shown initially; search for a specific item or choose Show all. Records → Items provides the complete inventory and its storage paths.</p>
  <p>World map starts with collapsed root locations. Expand into sublocations, present cast and stored possessions, or search and focus the current scene. Select a node to open its existing editor beside the map. Saved setups have Explore setup map; their edits stay in the setup draft until Save setup draft. Diagram cards can be moved with their Move button or arrow keys; positions stay in this browser. Change physical placement in the editor and save explicitly. Background and future tasks are labeled references. Add to scene references is a separate context choice; inspecting a node does not add it to the prompt or give a character knowledge. List provides the same records without dragging.</p>
  <p>Use Prepare scene in a saved setup or written story to request a scene such as a feeding round. It previews reused records, missing places/items, open tasks, background and a brief for the next passage. Review before Apply scene preparation. Tasks are open story threads; applying adds author-supplied world details without moving anyone, completing tasks or writing prose. New supplies are counted until you explicitly choose Always available in their item editor.</p>
  <p>Dev → Lore shows every background entry, its linked current records and storage paths, stock/supply mode, duplicates and wording flags. Preview lore selection uses the real context builder without a model call. Wording flags are review hints, not proof of contradictions. Save background edits explicitly; Load latest saved story and Reset help resolve concurrent changes. Setup → Background also offers structural diagnostics before chapter one.</p>
  <p>Contents opens earlier passages; continuation always happens at the latest. Ask and Review continuity discuss saved material without changing it. Review is advisory and may miss issues. Check its source excerpts and coverage.</p>
  <p>Change what happens replaces only the latest passage and its world effects. Polish wording compares an edit before you apply it. Records corrects current cast, places, possessions, relationships and threads; a canon correction pauses event replacement until you continue.</p>
  <p>The Writing guide in New story or Settings → Story sets pacing, dialogue and description preferences separately from content boundaries. Character profiles have a Current state note for temporary feelings and immediate intentions; enduring personality and history stay separate. Scene direction can apply to the next passage, the current scene or ongoing writing. Current scene remains active until you choose End scene; next-passage direction clears after an accepted continuation.</p>
  <p>In Records → Items, the Inventory browser groups possessions by their holder and shows what is inside each bag or crate. Mark a single item as Container, then choose it under another item's Owner / storage. Moving a container carries its contents; empty it before removing it or turning off Container. Only items held directly by a cast member can be equipped. Split this stack moves part of a saved stack while preserving the total; save or discard other edits first, then choose the quantity and destination and use Split and save.</p>
  <p>Pins protects selected saved fields across cast, places, items, relationships, threads and world notes from generated edits. Manual edits remain available in Records and update the value the pin protects. Add established world rules there to keep them in every story request. Pins and rules always receive context space; generation stops if they cannot fit. Rules guide the narrator, so still review the prose for mistakes.</p>
  <p>New story → Pins &amp; rules offers the same controls before chapter one. Save them with the setup draft; they are included in setup ZIPs and carry into the written story. Targeted AI edits respect those pins. Remove individual field pins before replacing the whole cast/world or removing a pinned setup record. World rules alone can stay during whole cast/world regeneration. Write or remove empty rules before saving or generating.</p>
  <h2>Illustrate your novel</h2>
  <p>Choose Illustrate this passage or create a portrait from a cast photobook. Character creation, story narration and pictures share a profile editable in setup, Records or the reader notebook. It separates actual/apparent age, identity/presentation, personality/voice/history, clothing by body region and current injuries; summaries update from those fields. Personality redesign preserves physical facts unless explicitly allowed. New pictures use apparent age and visible presentation, with natural-language prompts, framing and lighting. Fill missing details proposes undecided features while keeping entered fields; Remember visual identity saves reviewed features for future pictures. Refresh prompt from story updates the scene and shot-specific details. Review the resolved prompt, generate, then keep the result. Tagged crew pictures appear in each depicted character’s photobook. Older pictures remain when the current portrait or passage changes. Reference-image selections are stored for future workflows; current generation uses text prompts.</p>
  <p>In Illustrations, each character has image position, depth and gaze choices plus switches for which visual details enter the prompt. These choices survive story refresh and shot reuse. Places and items have a reusable Visual description for pictures in setup and Records; leave it blank to use their ordinary description. Review the resolved prompt before generating.</p>
  <h2>Keep your work</h2>
  <p>Saved records and accepted generations live locally. Form typing needs its Save action. Export ZIP in Library includes the complete saved story and accepted images; import makes a separate copy. Export novel downloads the written chapters as Markdown for reading or sharing, with hidden notes and internal records excluded. Keep ZIP backups before deleting a story.</p>
  <p>Unfinished stories have separate Export setup ZIP and Import a setup ZIP controls in Library. These include a premise with or without its cast/world plan. In New story, Save &amp; export setup ZIP saves current edits before downloading. Imports open as independent editable setups without calling AI or writing a chapter. Once chapter one is written, use the story ZIP instead.</p>
  <p class="muted">This instance uses {instance==='play'?'data/play on port 5175':'data/dev on port 5173'}. API keys stay in local server settings and are excluded from story exports. Development save migrations are not promised.</p>
  <h2>If generation stops</h2>
  <p>When prose is complete but its world updates fail, Recover a generated passage appears in the setup editor or reader. It keeps the exact prose on this computer, including after a restart. Review the issues, saved records and attempted changes. Correct and save any mistaken records, then choose Retry world updates. This uses the same prose with your latest saved facts. Once checks pass, review and choose Save recovered passage. You can also copy or discard the retained draft.</p>
  <p>If you add guidance after a successful check, retry to use those instructions or clear them before saving the earlier proposal. Recovery actions wait while the draft list reloads. If chapter one was saved in another window, Open saved story takes you to that result; any unsubmitted guidance remains available until you clear it or confirm leaving it behind.</p>
  <p>Accepted prose and world updates still save together. A real contradiction can keep a draft blocked; retries do not override pins or invent supplies. Changed written history or an invalidated rewrite recovery point prevents accepting that old draft, while its prose stays available to copy. Retained drafts are separate local recovery data and are excluded from story/setup ZIP exports. Failures before complete prose exists use the normal generation controls.</p>
  <p>The API requests strip at the bottom stays available while generation is running. Expand Requests for elapsed time, job phases, sent prompts/workflows and completed responses. The Errors button collects failed saves, form validation, browser errors and generation failures with credentials redacted. Dismiss one error, clear the list or copy it for troubleshooting. Opening the panel keeps your current screen in place.</p>
  <p>Your last completed save is kept. Check Settings service tests, then Dev → Jobs and Requests for the error. Reload before retrying an uncertain save. Text jobs interrupted by restart need an explicit retry; ComfyUI jobs reconcile with their original server when possible. Larger passage targets may need more output tokens and context.</p>
  <p>Memory uses current records, recent prose and source-linked summaries within a budget. It cannot guarantee every detail of a long novel. Treat recap/Ask/review as aids, correct mistaken current records, and inspect Dev → Memory/Context when coverage is incomplete.</p>
</main>
